{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1794552311,"nonce":"TU1fMCz3BNq9Vrx6KHJgE4R015k=","title":"A Rust side module aborts on an over-aligned allocation: hl_abi::ProgramAllocator refuses alignment above 16","message":"Reported by the hxn session (hxn issue 9ee1bd4): a Rust side module built for wasm32-wasip1-threads loads and runs under `ash run`, then aborts with \"memory allocation of 64 bytes failed\" and an `unreachable` trap once the module's thread pool starts. Their guess was missing thread setup (TLS, allocator registration).\n\nFindings, 2026-11-13, a reproduction in a scratch crate built with their flags (PIC, +atomics, +bulk-memory, -Z build-std, rust-lld -shared --shared-memory, the #[thread_local] errno that ash_hdll_channel uses), run with the release ash from 89001410:\n- The threads are not the cause. A primitive that starts 4 threads with `std::thread::spawn`, each making 1000 allocations of 64 bytes through ProgramAllocator, returns the right total (256000) under `ash run` on the threaded target.\n- The abort reproduces on the calling thread with no threads: `Box::new` of a `#[repr(align(64))]` struct of 64 bytes ends in rust_oom, abort and the same trap.\n- `ProgramAllocator::alloc` in hl_abi returns null for `layout.align() \u003e 16` (a deliberate refusal in its comment: wasi-libc's malloc aligns to 16). A thread pool allocates such blocks: crossbeam's CachePadded is 64-aligned on wasm, so a 64-byte padded counter is exactly \"64 bytes\". The pool is why it shows up when threads start, and why a build without atomics, which starts no pool, passes.\n- A patched copy of hl_abi (over-allocate by the alignment and a word, keep the block's address just below the aligned pointer, free that address in dealloc, copy in realloc) makes the same probe pass: 64-aligned boxes land on their boundary and the threads still return 256000. The diff is 64 lines against hl_abi at 5482332e; the verified copy and the diff are in the session scratchpad and were sent to hxn.\n\nSo the thread question: a module's own threads are ordinary `wasi.thread-spawn` threads. The host makes each one a second instance of the program, loads the side modules at the same table indices and gives each module's thread-locals a block from the program's malloc, filled from the first instance's initial image (ash_wasm_runtime/src/native/dylink.rs, `give_thread_block`). ProgramAllocator is wasi-libc's malloc and needs no per-thread registration.\n\nTo do:\n1. hl_abi: handle alignment above 16 in ProgramAllocator (patch above), or call posix_memalign if the runtime object is made to export it. hl_abi is a separate repository and I changed nothing in it.\n2. Ash has no in-tree test of a real Rust side module that starts threads: tests/side_module_threads.rs uses hand-written WAT with a bump allocator, and scripts/build_wasm_hdll.py builds only wasm32-wasip1. Add the probe as a test (needs nightly, rust-src and a PIC std, so it likely belongs behind a script and the wasm CI job, not the default test run).\n3. Not tested: a module's thread that calls HashLink allocation functions (hlp_alloc_bytes and the like). Ash registers GC mutators for threads it starts for Haxe; a pthread_create from inside a module is not registered, and whether an allocation from it is safe is unverified.","files":null}]}